{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall count for yourselves, etc.</b> Its saying <b>for yourselves</b> — because HaShem commanded to count seven Sabbaths, and they of blessed memory said (Zohar III:97a) that because they were in the impurity of Egypt, and HaShem wished to betroth Himself to this nation, He judged her according to the law of a menstruating woman, whose law is to count seven clean days, and He commanded that they count seven weeks, and then they would be fit for their entry like a bride to the canopy. And although there it is seven days and here seven weeks, on account of the severity of the impurity, and also their being included in the collectivity of Israel, the perfect One assessed that thus is their law. And see what I explained in the verse (Exodus 19:1) ״בַּחֹדֶשׁ הַשְּׁלִישִׁי לְצֵאת״ etc. And this is the meaning of the verse's statement here <b>and you shall count for yourselves</b> — meaning, the reason for this counting is for your sake, for your purification, for without this HaShem would have immediately given them the Torah. And along this path the reason for <b>from the morrow of the Sabbath</b> and not from the day of the Sabbath itself is precisely understood, for so is the law of counting that all the days must be complete, and since the fifteenth of Nisan, which is the day of the Sabbath mentioned here, for part of the day they were still in the land of Egypt, therefore HaShem commanded to count from the morrow. And although this was at the Pesach of Egypt, according to this law it is done in that very season year after year, for thus is it done in the sod of HaShem, as is known to those who know grace. And according to the plain sense, it is a remembrance of the Exodus occurring on the day of the Sabbath, a remembrance of ״כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״ etc., and the discerning one will understand. And see what I explained in parashat Bereshit in the verse ״וַיְכַל אֱלֹהִים בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי״ (Genesis 2:2)." "And by way of remez, it hinted in its saying <b>and you shall count</b>, according to what they of blessed memory said, that the souls of the people of Israel are in the aspect of the Tablets, and through ailments of the soul and the impurity of abomination their light becomes mingled and darkened. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 32:2) that the Tablets were of sappirinon. Therefore it says <b>and you shall count for yourselves</b> — meaning, through this counting you illuminate yourselves like sappirinon." "<b>From the morrow of the Sabbath, from the day, etc.</b> Meaning, from now, before you enter the land — ״from the morrow of the Sabbath״; and corresponding to after their entry into the land it says <b>from the day of your bringing</b>, etc. And therefore it placed ״from the morrow of the Sabbath״ first, because it is a commandment that applies already then, unlike ״from the day of your bringing,״ which is after entry into the land."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:36.624976769Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}